==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|emblématique}}. See {{m|en|emblem}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ĕmb-lə-mă-tĭk}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Serving as, or relating to a [[symbol]], [[emblem]] or [[illustration]] of a [[type]]
# [[typical|Typical]]; [[representative]].
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|title=Women’s World Cup 2015: England beaten by France in Group F opener|work={{w|The Guardian}} (London)|date=9 June 2015|url=http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/jun/09/france-england-womens-world-cup-group-f-match-report|passage=With Le Sommer withdrawn, France – now with five in midfield – seemed content to hold on for a deceptively narrow win. Such containment tactics can prove high risk but the only threat England could muster was a shot from Aluko which flew high and wide. It seemed entirely '''emblematic''' of their afternoon.}}
#* '''2019''', Oliver Norgrove, [https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2019/mar/13/cake-unicorns-brexit-zealots Hypnotised by cake and unicorns, the Brexit perfectionists have blown it] in the Guardian
#*: The result of yesterday’s meaningful vote in the House of Commons, much like the first in January, was '''emblematic''' of a very striking Brexit reality: that the principle barrier to leaving the EU comes from the very people most desperate to see it happen.

